course: everyday
lesson: 67
title: Allergies, horses and farm animals
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Some people's bodies react to animals: sneezing and itchy eyes near a cat often come from an allergy to tiny proteins in pet skin flakes and saliva, and no dog breed is completely free of them.
explain: A few people are so allergic to a bee or wasp sting that their throat can swell and they struggle to breathe; this is called anaphylaxis, and it is an emergency.
explain: Big farm animals are strong and easily frightened. Horses startle at sudden noise, cows protect their calves from dogs, and a bull can be very dangerous, so in the countryside you respect fences, follow the farmer's rules and keep your distance.
explain: Farm animals have their own diet, and the wrong food given over a fence can make them very ill.
explain: A family from London is walking across a field with their dog when the cows start to run at them. The dad lets go of the lead: the cows were after the dog, the dog easily outruns them, and the family walks calmly to the gate.
explain: A boy in Ohio with an allergy to cats stayed the night at a friend's house with three cats; his chest got tight and he needed his inhaler and a doctor.
explain: A girl in Kentucky fed a horse from her fingers instead of a flat hand; it nipped her by mistake and her finger was badly bruised.
fact: Many people are allergic to proteins in pet skin flakes and saliva, which cause sneezing and itchy eyes.
fact: No dog is completely allergy-free; some breeds shed less, but all dogs make the proteins people react to.
fact: An allergic child visiting a home with a pet tells the parent first and can bring their medicine.
fact: Even fur on clothes can make an allergic person ill, so you wash your hands after touching a class pet.
fact: Anaphylaxis is a dangerous allergic reaction, for example to a sting or to a food such as peanuts; you call the emergency number at once and help the person use their adrenaline pen if they have one.
fact: You feed a horse only with the owner's permission, holding your hand flat with the food on your palm.
fact: You do not feed horses in fields or climb into their field without the owner.
fact: When passing a horse and rider you slow down, give lots of room and say hello so the horse knows you are a person.
fact: Horses can doze standing up because their legs lock, but they lie down for deep sleep.
fact: If cows chase you because of your dog, you let go of the lead and get to safety.
fact: You never enter a field with a bull in it.
fact: A sheep stuck on its back cannot get up and can die, so you tell the farmer.
fact: Pigs are clean, clever animals; they cannot sweat, so they roll in mud to cool down and protect their skin from sunburn.
fact: Animal allergies can trigger asthma attacks that make breathing very hard; a child with asthma uses his inhaler and tells an adult.
fact: At a petting farm you hold a chick gently in cupped hands while sitting down, because a fall can hurt it.
fact: Horses cannot see well right in front of their noses, so they can bite fingers by mistake.
exercise: Every time you stroke your friend's cat you sneeze and your eyes itch. What should you do? | Tell an adult, you may be allergic | Stroke it more so you get used to it | Rub your eyes hard to stop the itch | Ignore it, it is just dust in the air
exercise: What causes most people's allergies to pets? | Proteins in pet skin flakes and saliva | The colour and length of the pet's fur coat | The pet's loud noises | The smell of the pet food in its bowl
exercise: You are allergic to dogs and invited to a friend's house with a dog. What should you do? | Tell your friend's parent before you come | Say nothing about it and just hope for the best | Stay home and never visit friends | Ask them to give the dog away before you visit
exercise: A shop says its puppies cause no allergies at all. What is true? | No dog is completely allergy-free | Some dogs never cause any allergies | Only small, fluffy dogs cause allergies | Only young puppies are allergy-free
exercise: A classmate is allergic to the class guinea pig. How can you help? | Wash your hands after touching it and keep fur away from them | Bring the guinea pig over so it can sit on their lap and they get used to it | Hide some fur in their pencil case | Tell them to stop being allergic
exercise: A friend who is allergic to bees is stung and starts to struggle to breathe. What should you do? | Call the emergency number and help them use their adrenaline pen | Give them a glass of cold water and wait quietly to see if it passes | Tell them to lie down and sleep | Put ice cream on the sting
exercise: How should you give a horse an apple? | Ask the owner, then hold it on your flat palm | Hold it tightly in your fingertips near its lips | Throw it into the horse's mouth | Put it between your teeth and lean in close to the horse
exercise: You walk past a field of horses by a footpath. What should you do? | Not feed them or climb into the field | Climb in to stroke them | Feed them grass cuttings from your garden | Wave a bag to make them run
exercise: You are riding your bike and meet a horse and rider on a lane. What should you do? | Slow down, give lots of room and say hello | Ring your bell loudly and race past as fast as you can | Ride close behind the horse | Stop suddenly and shout
exercise: Do horses sleep only standing up? | No, they doze standing but lie down for deep sleep | Yes, they never lie down at all, not even once in their lives | Yes, lying down would hurt them | No, they sleep only upside down
exercise: Cows start chasing you and your dog across a field. What should you do? | Let go of the lead and get to safety | Hold the dog tight on its lead and stand very still | Pick up the dog and run with it | Shout and run straight at the cows
exercise: There is a bull in the field your path goes through. What should you do? | Not enter the field | Walk through quickly and quietly | Climb in and wave to calm it | Throw it some bread to distract it
exercise: Can you feed farm animals bread from your lunch over the fence? | Only feed what the farmer gives you | Yes, bread is good for all farm animals | Yes, if you break it into small pieces | Yes, but only to the little baby animals
exercise: You see a sheep lying on its back with its legs in the air. What should you do? | Tell the farmer or an adult | Leave it, it is just sunbathing | Climb in and sit on it to help | Take a photo of it and walk on
exercise: How should you hold a chick at the farm? | Gently in cupped hands, sitting down | By one leg, standing up | Squeezed tight so it cannot run off | High up above your head so it can see around
exercise: Why do pigs roll in mud? | They cannot sweat, so mud cools them and stops sunburn | They are dirty animals that like mess | They are looking for food hidden under the mud, like worms and roots | Mud makes their skin grow hair
test: A girl in Colorado is told a certain fluffy dog breed is safe for her allergy. What should she know? | Some breeds shed less, but no dog is completely allergy-free | That breed can never cause sneezing or itchy eyes in anyone, ever | Allergies only come from cats | Fluffy dogs never have proteins
test: At a picnic a boy stung by a bee has a swelling face and trouble breathing. Why must someone call the emergency number at once? | It may be anaphylaxis, a dangerous allergic reaction | A bee sting always hurts for a short time | Bees are protected and every sting must be reported to the police | He needs a plaster for the sting
test: Why should you hold your hand flat when feeding a horse? | So the horse does not bite your fingers by mistake | So the horse sees the food better | Horses only eat from flat things like plates and trays | So the food does not fall
test: A family on a walk in Yorkshire sees a big bull standing in the field they want to cross. Which is the safe choice? | Find another way round and stay out of the field | Cross fast while it is looking away | Walk straight through the middle, holding the dog close | Shout and wave so the bull moves away
test: A boy feeds lawn clippings to horses over a fence because he thinks it is just grass. Why is this a problem? | The wrong food can make horses very ill | Horses only eat carrots and apples | It is too much food for one day | Lawn grass is too short for horses to bite and chew
test: A cyclist zooms past a horse without warning and the horse jumps sideways. Why did this happen? | Sudden noise and movement scare horses | Horses do not like the bright colour of bikes | The horse wanted to race against the bike | The horse was falling asleep on its feet
test: Someone says pigs are dirty because they love mud. Which is true? | Pigs are clean and clever; mud cools them because they cannot sweat | Pigs roll in mud because they enjoy being dirty and smelly all the time | Pigs need mud to grow their teeth | Mud makes pigs smell nice
test: A girl at a petting farm wants to walk around holding a chick. What is the safer way? | Sit down and hold it gently in cupped hands | Hold it high up so it can see everything around | Carry it in her pocket | Hold it by its wing
test: Cows run at a woman walking her dog on a lead. Why is letting go of the lead safer for her? | The cows want the dog, and the dog can outrun them | The cows will always stop running if they see a lead | Dogs scare cows away by barking | Letting go of the lead makes the cows lie down
test: aunty says her poodle is allergy free so i'll be fine. right? | No dog is fully allergy free, so be careful | Yes, poodles never cause allergies | Yes, small dogs are always safe | Yes, if it has been bathed today
test: friend ate a peanut, her face is swelling n she cant breathe proper | Call the emergency number now and help with her adrenaline pen | Give her a glass of water and wait for it to go down by itself | Let her lie down and have a nap | Tell her to cough it up herself
test: horse in the field looks hungry, can i give him my apple | Only with the owner's permission, on a flat hand | Yes, horses can eat anything you have | Yes, hold it in your fingers for him to nibble | Yes, throw it over the fence to him
test: cows comin at us cos of the dog!! what do i do | Let go of the lead and get to safety | Hold the dog tight and stand very still | Pick the dog up and run straight at them | Shout and wave your arms at all the cows
test: friend's got cats, i'm allergic, staying over anyway | Take your medicine and tell the adults first | Do not mention it to anyone, it will be fine | Just stay away from one cat | Pet them so you get used to it
test: gonna give the horse a carrot with my fingers | Hold it on a flat hand, fingers together | Hold it by the tip with your fingers | Throw it at the horse's face | Hold it high above its head
test: chest's tight and wheezy after patting the dog | Use your inhaler if you have one and tell an adult | Run round the garden fast to clear your lungs | Pat the dog more to get used to it | Hold your breath until it goes
test: heard the class hamster made a girl's face swell up and she couldn't breathe, they called an ambulance | Real lesson: anaphylaxis is an emergency; call the emergency number at once and help with the adrenaline pen | Real lesson: every class pet should be got rid of straight away, because all pets make children ill | It proves hamsters are poisonous | It is gossip, so allergies are never serious
test: my aunty says their poodle's 100% allergy-free so nobody can react to it | Not true; no dog is completely allergy-free, though some breeds shed less | She is right, poodles never cause allergies in anybody, whatever their breed or age | She is right, if it is washed weekly | She is right, small dogs are always safe
test: Leo sneezes round dogs and the kids say he's faking to get attention, pass it on | Do not pass it on; pet allergies are real and the rumour is unkind | Pass it on, because everybody knows sneezing round dogs is fake | Pass it on, so he stops | Pass it on, and add he cried
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the cause of allergy to pets? | Proteins present in pet skin flakes and saliva | The colour of the pet's fur | The pet's diet of meat | The noise made by the pet
exam: It being given that a person suffers anaphylaxis, select the course of action consistent with established guidance. | To call the emergency number at once and to assist with the adrenaline pen | To give the person a glass of water and to wait until the swelling subsides | To let the person lie down and sleep | To give the person a cold shower
exam: Identify the manner of feeding a horse when permitted by the owner. | With the hand held flat, the food resting upon the palm | With the food held firmly between the fingertips | By throwing the food over the fence | By placing the food directly into the horse's ear
exam: Select the response that is consistent with established guidance when passing a horse and rider. | To slow down, give ample room and greet the rider | To run past as fast as possible | To shout to make the horse move | To approach from behind silently
